🚀 Kubernetes ConfigMaps vs. Secrets — What’s the Difference?
When working with Kubernetes, storing configuration is common. But should you use a ConfigMap or a Secret? 

🔹 ConfigMap
Stores non-sensitive data (e.g., app settings, URLs).
Data is stored in plain text.
Best for environment variables, config files, or command-line arguments.

🔹 Secret
Stores sensitive data (e.g., passwords, tokens, certificates).
Data is base64 encoded (not encrypted by default).
Can be mounted as files or injected as env variables.

⚠️ Pro Tip:
Always enable encryption at rest for Secrets in Kubernetes.
Use external secret managers (Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, etc.) for production-grade security.
